from fastapi import FastAPI
from pydantic import BaseModel

- FastAPI creates API endpoints.
- BaseModel defines the structure of data that your API expects.
For example, an online shop item might have a name, price, and offer status. Pydantic helps validate that incoming data matches the expected structure and types.

app = FastAPI()
- This creates FastAPI application.

class Item(BaseModel):
    name: str
    price: float
    is_offer: bool | None = None 
- defining what information an item must contain.
- None means no value was supplied.
= None makes the field optional, so the client doesn't have to provide it.

@app.get("/")
def read_root():
    return {"Hello": "World"}
- @app.get("/") tells FastAPI to handle GET requests sent to the root URL /.
- def read_root(): defines the Python function that runs when that endpoint is called.
- return {"Hello": "World"} returns a Python dictionary, which FastAPI sends as JSON.

@app.get("/items/{item_id}")
def read_item(item_id: int, q: str | None = None):
    return {"item_id": item_id, "q": q}
this endpoint has two different kinds of parameters.
Path parameter: item_id

http://127.0.0.1:8000/items/5?q=laptop

The q value comes after ?. It is a query parameter.
q is the parameter name.
str means its value is text.
None and the default None mean it can be omitted.

@app.put("/items/{item_id}")
def update_item(item_id: int, item: Item):
    return {"item_name": item.name, "item_id": item_id}

@app.put("/items/{item_id}")
This registers a PUT endpoint. For example, a client can send a PUT request to /items/5.
item_id: int
FastAPI reads the item ID from the URL path and validates that it's an integer.
item: Item
This tells FastAPI to expect a JSON request body matching your Item Pydantic model.
